#!/bin/sh
# `game-play DIRECTORY|ZIP`: run the game importer (game-import.py) on a
# path and start the matching player.
case "${1:-}" in
    --help)
        cat <<'EOF'
Usage: game-play DIRECTORY|FILE

Find the games in a directory or ZIP, and start one with its player.

game-play inspects a game's files without running them and finds what can
play them: DOS programs, for dos-play(1); NES, SNES, Mega Drive, Master
System and Game Gear ROMs, for retro-run(1); and games ScummVM detects, for
scummvm(1). Only players installed on the machine are offered.

A DIRECTORY is inspected in place. A FILE, such as a ZIP or a single game
file, is copied into a new import directory under
$XDG_DATA_HOME/kandelo/game-imports (by default
~/.local/share/kandelo/game-imports), where each ZIP is also extracted
beside it, into NAME.unpacked. The copy stays there afterwards, so the
game can be played again from it. An import may hold at most 1,024 entries
and 512 MiB, 16 directory levels deep; links, special files, encrypted
ZIPs and ZIP64 archives are refused. For a DIRECTORY, a directory under
game-imports holds only the configuration ScummVM's detection writes; it
is the same for every run on that DIRECTORY and replaced each time.

When exactly one game is found, it is started, unless it is a DOS program
whose kind is uncertain: a .COM or .BAT file, or an LE/LX program without a
known DOS extender. Otherwise game-play lists what it found and starts the
one whose number is entered at the terminal; 0 cancels. Choosing needs a
terminal on standard input. game-play asks the page showing the machine to
open a terminal running the chosen game's command, with
kandelo-page-terminal(1); when no page takes the request, or the machine
has no kandelo-page-terminal, it runs the game itself.

The exit status is 1 when the input cannot be imported or inspected, or
contains no game it can play; otherwise it is 0, or the game's own status
when game-play runs the game itself.

      --help     display this help and exit
      --version  output version information and exit
EOF
        exit 0
        ;;
    --version) echo 'game-play (Kandelo game-import) 0.1.0'; exit 0 ;;
esac
export PYTHONHOME=/usr
exec /usr/bin/python3 /usr/libexec/kandelo/game-import.py play "$@"
